#
# The applications this effect recognizes, and what it does for each of them.
#
# This file is the effect's own defaults. It is installed with the package,
# replaced whenever the package is updated, and never written to. A user's own
# entries and changes live in the file of the same name under their
# configuration directory, where KConfig layers them over these: a field the
# user did not change keeps following this file, so a later package delivers
# new applications and corrected methods without touching what they edited.
#
# Every field below was read off a running instance of the stated version. A
# name never implies an identity, and a method is not a preference but a
# statement about how that program decides what to render, taken from its
# source and then confirmed by running it. Setting a method a program does not
# follow does not make it render less; it can stop its window covering the
# screen.
#
# An entry is found by two gates. Executable is the program's full path, as
# KWin resolves it for the window or, before any window exists, for the
# connection. WindowClass and Instance are the window's identity. Each field
# has a companion ExecutableMatch, WindowClassMatch or InstanceMatch: Exact,
# which is also what an absent one means, Substring or RegularExpression, the
# three KWin's own Window Rules offer. A regular expression has to match the
# whole value. An entry matches when every field it states matches.
#
# An entry that states a window identity cannot be known to match before the
# window exists, so it cannot answer when a program first asks about its
# screen. The entries below whose method is an advertisement therefore state
# the executable alone, as the file name in any directory; the window identity
# measured with them is recorded beside it.
#
# There is one Method entry per way the application can present itself, and
# each is a separate measurement: MethodWaylandFullScreen,
# MethodWaylandBorderless, MethodWaylandWindowed, MethodX11FullScreen,
# MethodX11Borderless and MethodX11Windowed. An entry that is absent follows
# the user's global method, which is Auto unless they chose another - nobody has
# run the application that way yet - and that is not the same as Off, which
# records that the question was asked and the answer is to ask for nothing. Each name below acts on one protocol only: the advertisements reach
# a native Wayland client, and the resize reaches an X11 window.
#
# Method is one of:
#   Auto                   Work it out and verify the result.
#   Off                    Recognize it, ask for nothing.
#   AdvertisedMode         Tell it alone that its screen has a smaller mode.
#                          For clients that select a mode and present it
#                          through a viewport still covering the screen.
#   AdvertisedScale        Tell it alone that its screen has a smaller scale.
#                          For clients that render the logical size times the
#                          scale they were told. Only whole steps of the
#                          output's scale are reachable, so an unscaled screen
#                          offers such a client nothing.
#   AdvertisedModeAndScale Both together, for clients that take their size from
#                          the mode but declare the output's scale themselves.
#   X11Resize              Give a selected X11 window a smaller drawable and
#                          present it at the size the system gave the window;
#                          the window itself keeps its place and size. Requires
#                          a client which follows resizing and RandR modes.
#
# Every other setting the effect has may also appear here, and each one absent
# follows the global value the user chose. This file states only what was
# measured about the application, never what somebody would prefer: a
# Resolution here would pin that game and stop the user's own global setting
# ever reaching it. So no entry states one, the benchmarks included.

[General]
FormatVersion=1

[Application-supertuxkart]
Name=SuperTuxKart
MeasuredVersion=1.4
# Its window: class supertuxkart, instance supertuxkart.
Executable=.*/supertuxkart
ExecutableMatch=RegularExpression
MethodWaylandFullScreen=AdvertisedMode
Order=10
Note=Follows the advertised screen mode in exclusive fullscreen, which its OpenGL renderer always uses. In the borderless fullscreen its Vulkan renderer uses by default it follows the surface scale instead.

[Application-extremetuxracer]
Name=Extreme Tux Racer
MeasuredVersion=0.8.4
# Its window class carries its version number, so matching the class would
# stop matching at the next package. The instance name is the stable field.
# Its program is etr. Not stated: on KWin 6.3 an X11 window's program comes
# from the PID the client reports, which has not been observed for this game,
# and a path that does not resolve would stop the entry matching at all.
Instance=etr
MethodX11FullScreen=X11Resize
X11PrimaryOutputOnly=true
X11RequiresEmulatedMode=true
Order=20
Note=Follows X11 resize requests on the primary display. Its SFML mode selection always uses that display, so requests on other displays are refused. Requested sizes must match an available mode. Its selected mode confirms that the game handled the resize; a smaller window alone can leave its rendering at the old resolution.

[Application-left4dead2]
Name=Left 4 Dead 2
MeasuredVersion=Steam build 23990068
# hl2_linux is the Source engine binary, not the game: every native Source
# title presents this class and instance, and runs a program of that name. The
# folder the program is in is what names the game, in whichever Steam library
# it is installed, so that is what the program pattern states. The window
# title also names the game, but a title carries save-game and player names and
# is never used as an identity here.
# To be confirmed on a running game: an X11 window's program comes from the PID
# the client reports, and whether that PID means the game out here under
# Steam's pressure-vessel runtime has not been observed. Where it does not, the
# path does not resolve and this entry does not match.
Executable=.*/Left 4 Dead 2/hl2_linux
ExecutableMatch=RegularExpression
WindowClass=hl2_linux
Instance=hl2_linux
MethodX11FullScreen=X11Resize
Order=50
Note=Takes its fullscreen size from the window manager and never asks Xwayland for a mode, so the effect presents the resized window itself and maps pointer input to it. Measured through Steam's pressure-vessel runtime.

[Application-wreckfest]
Name=Wreckfest
MeasuredVersion=Steam build 16986367
# A Windows game run through Proton, which sets the window's class and
# instance to steam_app_ and the game's Steam ID: 228380. That is the identity:
# the PID its windows report is Wine's explorer.exe /desktop, run by Proton's
# wine64-preloader, which every Proton game shares. Read off the running game
# with Proton Experimental on 2026-09-21. No method is stated: the X11
# presentation has not been measured, so it follows the global method.
WindowClass=steam_app_228380
Instance=steam_app_228380
Order=60
Note=Recognized by the window class Proton gives it. In exclusive fullscreen Wine keeps its window at the monitor's size and ignores resize requests, so it still supplies the full resolution.

[Application-glmark2]
Name=glmark2
MeasuredVersion=2023.01
# Its window: class com.github.glmark2.glmark2, instance glmark2-wayland.
Executable=.*/glmark2-wayland
ExecutableMatch=RegularExpression
MethodWaylandFullScreen=AdvertisedScale
Order=30
Note=Follows the advertised screen scale. A screen without a scale above one offers it no reduction.

[Application-vkmark]
Name=vkmark
MeasuredVersion=2025.01
# Its window: class com.github.vkmark.vkmark, instance vkmark.
Executable=.*/vkmark
ExecutableMatch=RegularExpression
MethodWaylandFullScreen=AdvertisedModeAndScale
Order=40
Note=Follows the advertised screen mode and scale together. A screen without a scale above one offers it no reduction.
